dsh: reasoning:
Very interesting. Now let me analyze the SET direction logic carefully, because this could be a place where the rev29 "correction" is itself wrong.

Definitions:
- Parent (F1 canonical): gamma is visible iff ∃q ∈ I+, ∃t0: tail γ([t0,T)) ⊆ J⁻(q) ∩ M. Call this predicate P.
- Variant SET: gamma is visible iff γ([0,T)) ⊆ ⋃_{q∈I+} J⁻(q) ∩ M. Call this predicate Q.

Note the negation in the conclusion: "no visible singularity" = no γ is visible. The class asserts ¬(∃γ: visible(γ)) i.e. ∀γ: ¬visible(γ).

Now, relationship between P and Q: P ⟹ Q (if a tail is contained in a single J⁻(q), then the whole image... wait, careful: Q says the whole curve image γ([0,T)) is contained in the union. If the tail from t0 is in J⁻(q), the initial segment γ([0,t0]) is a compact set of points in M. Is each point in the initial segment in some J⁻(q')? Not necessarily for arbitrary q' — but if I⁺ is nonempty and the spacetime is asymptotically flat... Hmm. Actually for a point p in M, is p ∈ J⁻(q) for some q ∈ I⁺? Not necessarily; points behind a horizon (in B = M \ J⁻(I⁺)) are not. So P does not trivially imply Q if the initial segment of γ lies in B but the tail enters J⁻(q). Hmm, but if γ is future-inextendible causal of finite affine length, its end is singular; the relevant point is: the curve could start inside a black hole and end at I⁺? No — a causal curve can't leave a black hole region once inside (B is future-trapped in the sense that J⁺(B) ⊆ B... J⁻(I⁺) is past-closed? If q ∈ I⁺ and p ∈ J⁻(q), then J⁻(p) ⊆ J⁻(q), so J⁻(I⁺) is past-closed: it's a past set. Hence if a tail is in J⁻(I⁺), the whole curve from earlier times is also in J⁻(I⁺) — because the curve is causal and past-directed from the tail: if γ(t) ⪯ γ(t0) and γ(t0) ⪯ q then γ(t) ⪯ q. Yes! Causality gives p ⪯ q transitively. So if the tail is in J⁻(q), then the whole past of the tail is in J⁻(q), including the initial segment. Therefore P ⟹ Q. Good.

Converse Q ⟹ P? Q says the whole curve is in the union. Does that give a single q whose J⁻(q) contains a tail? Not in general (omega-chain witness). So SET's visibility predicate Q is weaker (more curves visible) than P. Therefore "no visible singularity" under SET (∀γ ¬Q(γ)) is STRONGER as a conclusion than "no visible singularity" under parent (∀γ ¬P(γ))? Wait: conclusion is ¬∃γ visible. Under parent: ∀γ ¬P(γ). Under SET: ∀γ ¬Q(γ). Since Q is weaker (Q holds for more γ), ¬Q is a stronger condition, so ∀γ ¬Q(γ) is stronger than ∀γ ¬P(γ). So the variant's conclusion (that the WCC conclusion holds in the SET sense) is STRICTLY STRONGER than the parent's.

Hmm wait, but the registry says "strength: strictly weaker than AF-WCC-VAC-GEN". Let me think about what "strength" of a variant means. The variant replaces the visibility predicate. Hmm. There are two ways to view strength: (a) strength of the visibility predicate (SET visibility is weaker/coarser: claims more geodesics visible), or (b) strength of the resulting censorship statement (no set-visible singularities is stronger).

The rev12→rev13 change: F1 line 72 "strictly STRONGER -> proved EQUIVALENT" (about tail vs whole-curve readings for causal geodesics); line 213 misclassification example removed; line 234 variant SET "strictly STRONGER -> strictly WEAKER".

Hmm, but the earlier report says: L-FORM-03 residual: "the same inverted SET direction survives at research_map/formulation_taxonomy.yaml:200 ..." — i.e., the lead claims the F0 canonical taxonomy line 200 says "The set-based reading ... is strictly stronger". Let's look: canonical taxonomy line ~200 says: "The set-based reading (gamma contained in the union of J^-(q) over all q in I+) is strictly stronger; it is registered as variant SET". Hmm — is that "inverted"? Depends whether "reading" refers to the visibility predicate or the conclusion. The set-based *reading of visibility* is weaker as a predicate (more curves visible); as a censorship assertion it's stronger. The canonical F0 text says the set-based reading is "strictly stronger". Which sense? Given context: "The set-based reading ... is strictly stronger; it is registered as variant SET ... the two readings must never be interchanged." Hmm.

Actually wait. Let's reconsider. The variant's conclusion has "not exists set_visible_singularity" — the set-based no-visible-singularity assertion. If "the set-based reading" refers to the *conclusion* (the censorship statement), then it is strictly stronger. If it refers to the visibility predicate, it's strictly weaker. This ambiguity is exactly the kind of thing a checker should handle, and it's possible that both the canonical F0 (line 200) and the rev13 "correction" are talking past each other. That would be a genuine finding: the direction claim is ambiguous, not simply inverted.

Hmm, this is getting deep. Is that the best task for me though? Let me consider the risk: the lead-formulation already reported this as blocker L-FORM-03 with specific locations, and the interpretation "inverted SET direction" is their claim. If I independently verify and find that the canonical taxonomy's phrase is defensible (or ambiguous), I'd have a real finding that changes the disposition: L-FORM-03 may be a *wording ambiguity* rather than a factual inversion. That's high-value and bounded: read ~6 locations, formalize the implication in a small machine-checkable model (finite causal sets), and report.

Actually — even better and more rigorous: I can write a small executable witness that proves the implication direction: a finite "toy spacetime" (a finite poset with a distinguished I⁺ set) where:
- parent visibility P(γ) holds but SET visibility Q(γ) fails (proves P ⟹ Q strictly, i.e., Q does not imply P) — actually for a curve, if P holds then Q holds (proof via transitivity). A finite poset can witness separation in the other direction: a causal curve (chain) whose image is contained in the union of J⁻(q_i) but no tail is contained in any single J⁻(q) (omega-chain: γ = {x_1 > x_2 > ...} chain with x_i ∈ J⁻(q_i), and no q covers a tail). That's the omega-chain witness referenced (W076-GFORM-STRICTNESS-RECONCILE-06 T4).
- Then: the parent class (no P-visible singularity) vs SET variant (no Q-visible singularity): on the witness, Q(γ) holds and P(γ) fails, so the SET conclusion fails while parent holds → SET conclusion strictly stronger.

So the correct direction: as a visibility predicate, SET is strictly weaker; as a censorship conclusion, SET is strictly stronger. If a document says "the set-based reading is strictly stronger" without specifying, it is ambiguous; if it purports the predicate is stronger, inverted.

Hmm, hold on. Let me re-derive once more the sense in which "set-based reading" might be "strictly stronger". Consider the class WCC: "no visible singularity". Under the parent predicate, "not exists visible_singularity_from_I_plus" means no γ has a tail in a single J⁻(q). Under SET, "not exists set_visible_singularity" means no γ is entirely in J⁻(I⁺). The SET assertion implies the parent assertion (since P⟹Q, ¬Q⟹¬P). So SET's *conclusion* entails the parent's conclusion: SET ⊨ parent. A statement that entails another is stronger. So the SET *reading of the WCC conclusion* is strictly stronger. The variant's own `strength` field says "strictly weaker than AF-WCC-VAC-GEN" — which is about the visibility predicate. Ugh: the registry's phrase "(the parent's single-q tail predicate entails the union reading; the converse fails...)" — that's about predicates. So:

- Predicate strength: SET predicate is weaker (parent entails SET predicate).
- Conclusion strength: SET censorship conclusion is stronger (SET conclusion entails parent conclusion).

The registry `strength` field says "strictly weaker than AF-WCC-VAC-GEN" with parenthetical explanation about predicates — defensible if "strength of the visibility predicate". But the delta file's `strength` line says the same, and the canonical F0 line 200 says "The set-based reading ... is strictly stronger". So there's a real ambiguity/possible contradiction across documents. The lead's L-FORM-03 says lines in F0/FORMULATION/VARIANT_REGISTRY/SET-delta carry "the same inverted SET direction". But VARIANT_REGISTRY:57 and SET delta:11 now say "strictly weaker", so they've been corrected. The remaining ones are canonical taxonomy :200 ("strictly stronger") and supplement :176 — wait, supplement :176 is D1 item: "f0_reading: visibility = every incomplete causal geodesic lies outside J^-(I+) as a SET (strictly stronger)". Hmm, that D1 line says the F0 reading's *visibility* is "strictly stronger" — again the predicate sense, which is arguably inverted (the set-based visibility predicate is weaker). But it's describing the historical F0 reading.

So the actual finding I can make: the direction depends on whether "strength" attaches to the visibility predicate or to the censorship conclusion; the documents use both senses without disambiguating; at least the D1 line's "(strictly stronger)" attached to "visibility = ... as a SET" is inverted *in the predicate sense*, while the F0 line 200's phrase could be read in the conclusion sense. And critically, the rev29 repair's claim (3) that it corrected F1's line 234 to "strictly weaker" leaves an internal inconsistency: in the same SET variant, the visibility predicate is weaker, but the variant's conclusion ("no set-visible singularity") is stronger than the parent's. A reviewer must not flip the variant's conclusion strength. This is a *substantive* finding for r3: the direction correction is sense-dependent and the repair may have introduced a new conflation (the variant's conclusion now described via a "strictly weaker" predicate while the conclusion is strictly stronger).

Hmm, this is genuinely deep and useful. But is it too clever/risky? I can make it rigorous with a machine-checkable finite witness: build a finite causal set model with I⁺ and chains, run a script that:
